Traditionally, alignment methods have been based on RLHF, which typically involves training reward models using MLE and applying an RL algorithm to tune the LM to maximize rewards \citep{akrour2011preference,cheng2011preference,christiano2017deep,askell2021general,rame2024rewarded}. RLHF is often performed with on-policy methods such as REINFORCE \citep{sutton1999policy} or PPO \citep{schulman2017proximal},  but these have been shown to be computationally expensive and often unstable \citep{ouyang2022training}.

