\paragraph{Examining Performance on PYTHIA}We observe that one of the clear distinctions between techniques such as DPO, A-LOL, and KTO versus the others is a large performance gap on the PYTHIA models, across all evaluations. We believe that one of the main reasons for this performance gap is a tendency for the aforementioned techniques to ramble or hallucinate. To justify this hypothesis, we plot the length of the responses generated by each of these techniques on PYTHIA-6.9B relative to the length of the chosen response in \autoref{fig:length}. While SFT and UPO have roughly the same length (in expectation) as the chosen response, DPO, KTO, and A-LOL tend to have significantly longer responses (with their median being over 5x longer and A-LOL being more than 10x longer). In fact, roughly half the generations from these three techniques are between 5-100x longer than the chosen response.

However, despite these issues in the generations for PYTHIA models specifically, we do not observe any particular training instability (e.g., explosions in loss or gradient norm) during training; additionally, it is worth noting that DPO and KTO perform reasonably well on the GPT-4 evaluation for the LLAMA models, which indicates that this may be an issue isolated with the combination of these techniques with PYTHIA models only. Based on the results in \citet{ethayarajh2024kto}, they do not encounter similar issues with LLAMA-3 or other model architectures.

